= Nitrogen balance
{parent=Nutrition}

The difference between total nitrogen intake and loss over a stated interval, equal to the change of nitrogen retained in the body when all routes are counted.

= Dietary fibre
{parent=Nutrition}

= Dietary fiber
{synonym}

= Iodine deficiency
{parent=Nutrition}

Deficiency of <iodine> that can affect <thyroid> function.

= Malnutrition
{parent=Nutrition}

= Nutrient deficiency
{parent=Malnutrition}

= Vitamin C deficiency
{parent=Nutrient deficiency}

Insufficient <vitamin C> can produce the clinical syndrome <scurvy>. <Vitamin C Fact Sheet for Health Professionals by NIH>[Vitamin C Deficiency]{ref}

= Scurvy
{parent=Vitamin C deficiency}

A clinical consequence of <vitamin C> deficiency, with impaired <collagen> formation and fragile <connective tissue>. <Vitamin C Fact Sheet for Health Professionals by NIH>[Vitamin C Deficiency]{ref}

= Serum urate
{parent=Physiology}

= Physiological response
{parent=Physiology}

A functional change in an organism following a stimulus. The response can occur remotely through <reflexes> without transport of the original stimulus to the responding tissue.

= Gas exchange
{parent=Physiology}

= Oxygen saturation
{parent=Physiology}

= Mucus
{parent=Physiology}

= Lymphatic system
{parent=Physiology}

= Lymphatic drainage
{parent=Lymphatic system}

= Lymphatic vessel
{parent=Lymphatic system}

= Lymphatic valve
{parent=Lymphatic vessel}

= Lymphatic valves
{synonym}

A valve limiting backward movement of <lymph>.

= Thoracic duct
{parent=Lymphatic system}

The main lymphatic channel delivering intestinal <chyle> and other <lymph> to the left jugulosubclavian region.

= Cisterna chyli
{parent=Thoracic duct}

An inconstant enlarged origin of the <thoracic duct> in the upper lumbar region.

= Lacteal
{parent=Lymphatic system}

A central lymphatic capillary within an <intestinal villus>, receiving <chylomicrons> from intestinal absorption.

= Spleen
{parent=Lymphatic system}

= Splenic white pulp
{parent=Spleen}

= Lymphoid organ
{parent=Lymphatic system}

= Lymph node
{parent=Lymphatic system}

= Paracortex
{parent=Lymph node}

= Germinal centre
{parent=Lymph node}

A region of an activated secondary lymphoid follicle in which <B cells> proliferate and undergo selection.

= Mesenteric lymph node
{parent=Lymph node}

= Lymph
{parent=Lymphatic system}

= Chyle
{parent=Lymph}

Lipid-rich intestinal <lymph>.

= Urine
{parent=Physiology}

= Polyuria
{parent=Urine}

= Heart
{parent=Physiology}

A muscular pump supporting blood circulation. Electrical activity, mechanical contraction and <perception> of heartbeat-related signals are distinguishable measurements.

= Cardiac ventricle
{parent=Heart}

= Atrioventricular node
{parent=Heart}

= Pericardium
{parent=Heart}

= Heart valve
{parent=Heart}

= Heart valves
{synonym}

= Cardiac valves
{synonym}

= Cardiac valve
{synonym}

= Left ventricle
{parent=Heart}

= Cardiac output
{parent=Heart}

= Aortic valve
{parent=Heart}

= Cardiac cycle
{parent=Heart}

The recurrent sequence of contraction and relaxation during a heartbeat. Electrical activity, mechanical contraction and sensory afferent signals have related but different timings.

= Diastole
{parent=Cardiac cycle}

The relaxation and filling phase of a <cardiac cycle>. In cardiac-phase experiments it provides a comparison period with different <baroreceptor> signalling.

= Systole
{parent=Cardiac cycle}

The contraction phase of a <cardiac cycle>; ventricular <systole> includes ejection of blood. Timing a stimulus to <systole> can change its relation to cardiovascular afferent signals.

= Perfusion
{parent=Physiology}

Delivery of blood through a tissue. Changes in <perfusion> can influence functional imaging and must be separated from changes in the computation of interest.

= Pulmonary ventilation
{parent=Physiology}

= Breathing
{synonym}

Movement of air into and out of the lungs. Changes in breathing can influence cardiac timing and can accompany <emotion> or nonemotional physical demands. Breathing must therefore be measured or controlled when interpreting appropriate <psychophysiology> recordings.

= Apnoea
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

= Apnea
{synonym}

Suspension of <breathing>. In nineteenth-century accounts it can include the developing asphyxial condition; its meaning must be established from context rather than automatically identified with a modern sleep-disorder diagnosis.

= Alveolar ventilation
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

= Hyperventilation
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

= Artificial respiration
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

= Hering-Breuer inflation reflex
{c}
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

A lung-inflation <reflex> carried through <vagus nerve> sensory pathways that inhibits inspiration. It was described in 1868 by <Ewald Hering> and <Josef Breuer>, after the 1867 examination.

= Airway
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

= Aspiration
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

Entry of material into the <airways> from the mouth, <pharynx> or <stomach>.

= Tracheal cannula
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

A tube placed in the <trachea> to provide an airway or a connection to respiratory recording apparatus.

= Respiratory mechanics
{parent=Pulmonary ventilation}

<Respiratory mechanics> relates the movements of the <thorax> and <respiratory diaphragm> to <pressure> changes and airflow.

= Transpulmonary pressure
{parent=Respiratory mechanics}
{title2=$P_{\rm L}=P_{\rm alv}-P_{\rm pl}$}

Distending <pressure> difference between <alveolar pressure> and <pleural pressure>.

= Alveolar pressure
{parent=Respiratory mechanics}
{title2=$P_{\rm alv}$}

Air <pressure> inside a pulmonary <alveolus>.

= Pleural pressure
{parent=Respiratory mechanics}
{title2=$P_{\rm pl}$}

<Pressure> within the <pleural cavity>; it is distinct from <alveolar pressure>.

= Electromyography
{parent=Physiology}

= EMG
{synonym}
{c}

Recording electrical activity associated with <muscle> activation. Surface recording electrodes sample activity near the skin; electrode position and movement affect the signal.

= Motor-evoked potential
{parent=Electromyography}

= MEP
{c}
{synonym}

A muscle response following stimulation of a motor pathway. Its amplitude indexes excitability under specified recording conditions, rather than directly measuring the activity of a single <mirror neuron>.

= Facial electromyography
{parent=Electromyography}

Recording electrical activity over facial <muscle> regions, such as the cheek and brow. It can detect small facial responses that are not obvious to an observer. A response compatible with a smile or frown does not by itself establish the corresponding subjective <emotion>.

= Arousal
{parent=Physiology}

A state variable involving activation and responsiveness. Its sensory, motor, autonomic and cognitive components need not vary together, and it can confound an interpretation based on reward or threat alone.

= Physiological arousal
{parent=Physiology}

Bodily activation measured through specified physiological variables, such as <heart rate> or <skin> conductance. <Physiological arousal>, subjective distress and catastrophic interpretation are distinct measurements.

= Heart rate
{parent=Physiology}

= Heart rate variability
{parent=Heart rate}

Variation in the intervals between successive heartbeats. Its interpretation depends on the time scale, <breathing> and recording conditions; it is not a context-free measure of a particular <emotion>.

= Wound healing
{parent=Physiology}

Coordinated tissue repair involving growth, migration, matrix remodelling and immune responses. Persistent deployment of related activities can support a <tumour> without constituting productive repair.

= Scar
{parent=Wound healing}

= Granulation tissue
{parent=Wound healing}

= Electrophysiology
{parent=Physiology}

= Fast-scan cyclic voltammetry
{parent=Electrophysiology}

An electrochemical approach measuring rapid changes in oxidation/reduction currents. Chemical identification, calibration and electroactive interferents matter when interpreting a signal as a particular <neurotransmitter>.

= Physiological stress
{parent=Physiology}

A physiological response to challenge or disturbance. Its cause, duration and measured consequences should be specified rather than inferred from one behavioural change.

= Aerobic capacity
{parent=Physiology}

Capacity to sustain oxygen-dependent energy <metabolism>, commonly assessed through maximum oxygen consumption in a specified activity or condition.

= Muscle tissue
{parent=Physiology}

= Muscle cell
{parent=Muscle tissue}

= Smooth muscle cell
{parent=Muscle cell}

= Muscle atrophy
{parent=Muscle tissue}

= Muscle tone
{parent=Muscle tissue}

= Smooth muscle
{parent=Muscle tissue}

A contractile muscle class whose cells lack the conspicuous sarcomeric striations of skeletal and cardiac muscle. Differentiated smooth-muscle layers can also mechanically constrain neighbouring growing tissues.

= Digestive system
{parent=Physiology}

= Large intestine
{parent=Digestive system}

= Caecum
{parent=Large intestine}

= Faeces
{parent=Digestive system}

= Excretin
{parent=Faeces}

= Excretine
{synonym}

A historical name for a crystalline material isolated from human <faeces>. Early descriptions and formulas are not sufficient to establish that every preparation was one pure modern compound.

= Intestinal motility
{parent=Digestive system}

= Regurgitation
{parent=Digestive system}

= Lower oesophageal sphincter
{parent=Digestive system}

= Gastrointestinal tract
{parent=Digestive system}

The continuous digestive passage through which ingested material travels.

= Gallbladder
{parent=Digestive system}

= Belching
{parent=Digestive system}

= Stomach
{parent=Digestive system}

= Pylorus
{parent=Stomach}

= Gastric outlet obstruction
{parent=Stomach}

= Gastritis
{parent=Stomach}

= Gastric acid secretion
{parent=Stomach}

Production of the acidic component of gastric fluid. <Vagus nerve> activity is one of several regulatory influences.

= Digestion
{parent=Digestive system}

Processing <food> into forms suitable for use by an <organism>. Historical explanations by <digestive heat> must be distinguished from modern <physiology>.

= Lipid digestion
{parent=Digestion}

= Mixed micelle
{parent=Lipid digestion}

An aggregate containing amphipathic molecules such as <bile salts> and products of <lipid digestion>, helping deliver poorly water-soluble lipids to intestinal absorptive surfaces.

= Nutrient absorption
{parent=Digestion}

Transfer of digested <nutrients> across absorptive surfaces, especially the <small intestine>. This is distinct from optical absorption.

= Bile
{parent=Digestive system}

= Bile pigment
{parent=Bile}

Pigmented products associated with haem breakdown and <bile>, including <bilirubin>.

= Biliverdin
{parent=Bile pigment}

= Biliary fistula
{parent=Bile}

An opening through which <bile> drains by an abnormal or experimentally created route. Historical external collection was used to investigate <bile> secretion and digestive function.

= Bilirubin
{parent=Bile}

= Haematoidin
{parent=Bilirubin}

A historical tissue-pigment name associated with iron-free <bilirubin>, often encountered as crystals in older haemorrhages. Appearance alone need not establish the detailed composition of every tissue deposit.

= Bile salt
{parent=Bile}

= Taurocholic acid
{parent=Bile salt}

= Vomiting
{parent=Digestive system}

= Retching
{parent=Vomiting}

= Pregnancy-associated vomiting
{parent=Vomiting}

= Intestine
{parent=Digestive system}

The digestive-tract region containing specialized absorptive and secretory <epithelium>. Architecture differs between intestinal regions and species.

= Intestinal lumen
{parent=Intestine}

= Rectum
{parent=Intestine}

= Muscularis mucosae
{parent=Intestine}

= Lamina propria
{parent=Intestine}

= Peyer's patch
{parent=Intestine}
{c}

= Intestinal obstruction
{parent=Intestine}

= Colon
{parent=Intestine}

= Bowel
{parent=Intestine}

= Gut granule
{parent=Intestine}

A <lysosome>-related <organelle> of the <Caenorhabditis elegans> intestine. Loss of granule formation can alter a gut-marker assay without eliminating the intestinal founder lineage.

= Small intestine
{parent=Intestine}

= Ileum
{parent=Small intestine}

= Duodenum
{parent=Small intestine}

= Brunner gland
{c}
{parent=Duodenum}

= Brunner's gland
{c}
{synonym}

A predominantly mucous gland of the <duodenum> whose ducts communicate with <intestinal crypts>.

= Intestinal crypt
{parent=Intestine}

= Lieberkühnian gland
{c}
{synonym}

= Lieberkühn gland
{c}
{synonym}

An epithelial invagination housing proliferative populations in the adult mammalian intestinal lining. Crypt structure, supporting cells and developmental timing vary with region and species.

= Intestinal villus
{parent=Intestine}

= Intestinal villi
{synonym}

A projection of the intestinal lining that increases exposed absorptive surface. Mammalian small-intestinal villi must be distinguished from microscopic microvilli on individual epithelial cells and from the normally non-villous colon.

= Microbial digestion
{parent=Physiology}

Processing of food by microorganisms associated with a host. Measuring the resulting nutrients and their absorption helps establish the benefit to the host.

= Diet
{parent=Physiology}

The food consumed by an organism. Realized diet, short-term feeding observations and the diet inferred from inherited anatomy are different kinds of evidence.

= Wine
{parent=Diet}

= Sherry
{parent=Wine}

= Food
{parent=Diet}

= Bread
{parent=Food}

= Fallback food
{parent=Diet}

A food relied on when preferred resources are unavailable. Selection associated with difficult periods can shape anatomy without making that food dominant in short-term observations.

= Circulatory system
{parent=Physiology}
{wiki}

= Pulmonary circulation
{parent=Circulatory system}

= Arterial pulse
{parent=Circulatory system}

A travelling <pressure> disturbance produced by cardiac ejection in the coupled <blood> and arterial wall. Its propagation is distinct from the transport of individual blood cells.

= Pulse wave velocity
{parent=Arterial pulse}
{title2=$c_p=L/\Delta t$}

The propagation speed inferred from an effective path distance and the travel-time difference of corresponding features in two arterial waveforms. Path definition, synchronized timing and reflections matter; a pulse peak need not track an unchanged waveform.<2024 Recommendations for Validation of Noninvasive Arterial Pulse Wave Velocity Measurement Devices>[Pressure-waveform acquisition and transit-time estimation]{ref}

= Vein
{parent=Circulatory system}

= Vena cava
{parent=Vein}

= Dural venous sinus
{parent=Vein}

= Deep dorsal vein of the penis
{parent=Vein}

= Subclavian vein
{parent=Vein}

= Left subclavian vein
{parent=Subclavian vein}

= Azygos vein
{parent=Vein}

= Phlebitis
{parent=Vein}

= Pterygoid plexus
{parent=Vein}

= Cavernous sinus
{parent=Vein}

= Inferior ophthalmic vein
{parent=Vein}

= Superior ophthalmic vein
{parent=Vein}

= Venous valve
{parent=Vein}

= Venous valves
{synonym}

A structure within a <vein> that helps oppose backward movement of <blood>. <William Harvey> used observations of these structures and experiments with ligatures in his argument for <blood circulation>. <On the Motion of the Heart and Blood in Animals by William Harvey>[chapter XIII]{ref}

= Blood vessel
{parent=Circulatory system}

= Vasa vasorum
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Tunica adventitia
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Tunica media
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Tunica intima
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Internal pudendal vessels
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Obturator vessels
{parent=Blood vessel}

Arterial and venous vessels accompanying the <obturator nerve> through the <obturator canal>.

= Anastomosis
{parent=Blood vessel}

A connection between <blood vessels> through which flow can pass. In <angiogenesis>, joining sprouts helps turn isolated extensions into a connected circulation.<Angiogenesis in Inflammation and the Microcirculation>[Chapter 6, sprouting and connections between vessels]{ref}

= Aneurysm
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Femoral aneurysm
{parent=Aneurysm}

= Aortic aneurysm
{parent=Aneurysm}

= Vascular compliance
{parent=Blood vessel}
{title2=$C=dV/dP$}

Change in vascular volume per change in distending <pressure>; it describes storage rather than an independent source of <energy>.

= Artery
{parent=Blood vessel}

= Anterior spinal artery
{parent=Artery}

= Atherosclerosis
{parent=Artery}

Disease with plaque formation in arterial walls; plaque can restrict flow or provide a site of <thrombosis>.

